WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910
So, now you have a beautiful front-end

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.630
design and you know that the back end

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.510
works, that your website works. How do

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.230
you now take this file, which is

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.990
currently living just in your local

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.390
device, your own laptop, and actually

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.510
ship it online so that people can access

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.590
it? And just to show you what I mean, if

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.150
you've been following along, most likely

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.950
you will have this file as well, where

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.190
you have an HTML site that is currently

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.950
sitting somewhere in your file explorer,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.470
somewhere in your computer. So, our goal

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.790
for this section is to bring this file

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.430
over to the web so that people can

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.270
actually access it. And that is step

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.870
four of the Craft framework, which is

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.950
firing it up, deploying it so that it is

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.150
now live for its intended users. Now,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.430
for the other builds in the classroom,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.150
you'll see me show how we can set it up

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.910
so that it's behind a login if that's

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.950
needed, but since this is a website that

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.390
needs to be freely accessible to the

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.870
internet, then we'll just actually be

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.550
deploying this through a tool called

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.790
Vercel. And Vercel is an amazing tool,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.990
and it is also a free tool. You can see

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.470
I've been using it for a while in order

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.630
to deploy a lot of our websites and all

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.190
of the things and resources that you see

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.430
here, including the brand design

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.150
systems, including the color palette

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.470
tool kit, and all of this, I didn't

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.070
really go to this dashboard and deploy

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.310
it. I just had Claude do it for me. And

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.150
so, to do that, going back to our

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.150
session where we have that Agency X

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.910
website, what I'll just say is, "Hey

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.430
Claude, can you now deploy this to

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.190
Vercel?" And if you want, you can also

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.910
choose a name, and for this one I'm

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:16.190
saying

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.110
"robonuggetsagencyxdemo.vercel.app"

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.230
is the URL that I want. And for any

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.310
deployment, basically the process of

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.870
putting it out there, when you use

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.870
Vercel, it always ends with .vercel.app.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.990
Now, if you want it to be a an actual

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.470
TLD, a domain that ends in like .com or

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.870
.io or .ai, then I'll link a guide

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.830
below, which you can just go to and that

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.430
will just guide you through on how to do

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.910
that. And also, note to do this, you can

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.030
see I have this really small scale file

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.950
called Vercel that just teaches Claude

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.430
on how to do that. So, I'll link that

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.150
down as well below. And if in case it's

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.630
your first time using Vercel, it is

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.390
actually really easy. What Claude will

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.310
basically do is it will install the

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.030
Vercel CLI, the command line interface,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.470
and then it will just give you a link to

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.270
your browser where you can log in with

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.070
your Versel account. So, it's as easy as

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.030
that. And because Versel is very AI

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.750
agent forward, that's all the setup you

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.510
need. And from that point on, your

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.630
Claude can now access Versel and deploy

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.630
to Versel whenever you wish. And after

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.390
just a few seconds, you can now see that

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.870
it is now live on the URL slug that we

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.710
chose. And if I click on that, that will

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.790
now take us straight to the Agency X

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.910
website, and you can now access this

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.430
from all over the web. And you can see

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.070
it has all of that functionality and

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.870
that beautiful front-end interface that

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.230
we designed together. And now you have a

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.430
live agency website that you can just

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.910
provide to any prospective client and

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.870
actually let them contact you through

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.630
this form. And so, that brings us to the

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.750
final step of the Craft framework.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.070
Because now that we have deployed the

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.750
website, we are actually pretty much

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.030
done. But for any build or big task that

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.470
you do with Claude, it is actually a

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.190
good idea to let Claude document that

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.310
process so that you can turn it into a

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.150
skill that maybe next time you create a

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.390
website, you already have a skill that

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.110
you can just fire up and it will relearn

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.390
everything that you did during that

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.710
session, all of the mistakes that it

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.110
did, so that the next time you build

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.430
this piece of software or something

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.030
similar to it for a client or for

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.949
yourself, then you can do things faster

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:04.710
and also at a level of quality that

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.430
meets your expectations every time. And

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.190
also with that, you can tailor that

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.390
build for any client or for any build.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.630
Because now that you have standardized

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.030
that process, you can now flex that

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.870
process for any deliverable that you

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.550
need to make. And to do that is really

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.710
simple. So, in the session that you were

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.550
building the website, we can just say,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.190
"Hey Claude, so I want to create a

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.790
skill. Let's call it website creation

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.710
where I just want to note down and for

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.750
you to document the process in clear

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.870
simple steps on how we built out this

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.870
website, so that the next time we create

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.750
websites together, you will be able to

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.590
use this skill for best practices.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.470
Please make sure that you note all of

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.470
the things that I corrected you on, as

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.030
well as the feedback that I made

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.030
throughout the process." And usually

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.270
when building out an artifact like this,

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.550
I usually invoke this align skill in

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.030
order for me and Claude to just be

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.710
aligned on what exactly it will do. Now,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.310
the more important the build, the more

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.510
questions that I ask it to ask me. And

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.990
uh at least for this, since this is just

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.270
a simple skill, I just ask it to give me

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.510
three questions so that we are fully

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.190
aligned before it builds it out. But

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.710
it's just good practice, especially if

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.190
you're documenting something that is

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.949
going to be important for your business.

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:11.310
So now we'll just answer this and we'll

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.470
let it build the skill for us. So now as

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.150
you can see, we now have that website

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.110
creation skill created by Claude for us,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.870
and it captures all of the learnings and

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.110
all of the process improvements that you

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.510
just guided Claude throughout this

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.390
session. Now the follow-on prompt that I

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.590
sent after that first one, I also ask it

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.550
to please show it visually on Rubric

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.590
Flows, and I will link that below. That

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.590
is basically a tab in Rubric, which is

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.470
my agentic operating system. And just to

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.710
show you what it is, it is just a visual

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.230
layer where if you have a skill or an

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.990
automation that you are building out,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.150
let's say you're building this out for a

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.909
client, it is often the case that with

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:45.390
any client or any colleague that you're

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.070
dealing with, it's much better to show

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.190
things visually because a skill, under

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.110
the hood, it's just a markdown file,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.710
right? It's just a text file. So just

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.190
imagine if you were taking someone

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.350
through a skill file that you have built

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.830
for them or an automation that you have

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.510
built for them, then taking them through

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.870
like a notepad is probably not the best

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.830
way to do it. So this is just a great

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.190
way for you to visualize the flow of the

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.710
steps so that you can now tailor this to

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.670
any client's needs. So let's say if they

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.510
ask you what your process is with

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.310
building out a website, then you can

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.470
just quickly show it here in this nice

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.190
interactive view. You can actually ask

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.190
Claude as well to connect to Rubric

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.710
Flows and to this workflow in particular

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.350
in order to just simulate the different

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.230
steps that it will take for completing

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.110
that task, which in this case is

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.710
creating that website. So that's an

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.390
option for you to do if in case you want

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.230
to install and have access to Rubric,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.830
but generally the key principle that I

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.390
wanted to impart and teach is that if

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.750
you have a large build, you always want

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:41.790
to just make sure to create a skill for

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.990
that and create a repeatable process so

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.950
that the next time that you undertake

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.750
this same task, you're able to start off

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.360
strong.
